\textbf{Problem:} Find the matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $y$-axis.

\textbf{Solution:}
Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}$ be a vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$.  The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis is given by
\[ \text{proj}_{\mathbf{j}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{j}}{\|\mathbf{j}\|^2} \mathbf{j} \]
where $\mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$.  We have $\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{j} = x(0) + y(1) = y$ and $\|\mathbf{j}\|^2 = 0^2 + 1^2 = 1$.  Therefore,
\[ \text{proj}_{\mathbf{j}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{y}{1} \mathbf{j} = y \mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix}. \]
The matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $y$-axis is
\[ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. \]

\textbf{Answer:}
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}
